Interpretation: If c != null, then the new Vector is not equal to c. Equality defined by Vector.equals(Object o) which checks if other is a List and same elements. Since c is a Collection, not necessarily a List, but could be List. The spec says not equal. So assertion: (\old(c != null)?) Actually c is a parameter; its value is known at entry. Use condition c != null => !this.equals(c). But we cannot call this.equals(c) as it's allowed (public method). Use this.equals(c) is okay. However spec says "constructed collection is not equal to the source collection". So assert: (c != null) => !this.equals(c). Use => syntax.

Thus final assert: assert (c != null) => !this.equals(c);

